      "text": "The medical contribution identifies the need for further assessment, evaluates other conditions through appropriate examinations and tests, and coordinates a comprehensive evaluation. The slide lists multiple possible specialties, including developmental pediatrics, gastroenterology, ENT, pulmonology, neurology, dentistry, and others. Their presence on the list does not mean that every child needs every specialty.\n\nNutrition assessment asks whether intake supplies needed energy, fluid, macronutrients, vitamins, and minerals. The team may need to match nutritionally useful foods to the skills the child actually has, rather than judging adequacy from calories alone.\n\nDiet planning incorporates family lifestyle, religious and cultural beliefs, and tolerance. The nutrition contribution works with the physician, feeding specialist, family, and other team members. A diet can meet a calculated target yet still require adjustment if the child cannot manage it or the family cannot use it.\n\nThe deck describes ongoing monitoring and team collaboration when increasing oral intake and decreasing tube dependence. This is an assessment and coordination principle, not a self-directed tube-weaning protocol or a prescribed diet.",
