      "text": "Psychosocial assessment examines how both child and caregiver respond to the feeding disorder, what happens during meals, how caregivers respond to feeding behaviors, and why those behaviors occur. It also addresses participation and positive mealtime experiences using evidence-based strategies. It does not reduce refusal to blame or assume a behavioral cause from refusal alone.\n\nThe ST/OT slide describes overlapping team contributions: swallowing assessment collaboration, oral movement, whole-body coordination, fine motor control, posture, sensory experience, and comfort with textures. The combined heading is not an exclusive profession-by-profession scope rule.\n\nHead and trunk control provide a foundation for the movements involved in feeding. Eye, shoulder, arm, and hand coordination support utensil use and self-feeding. Oral-motor and sensory assessment ask different but potentially interacting questions: how the mouth moves and how the child responds to food experiences.\n\nA useful team question connects findings: can the child manage the proposed foods, is intake nutritionally adequate, are medical contributors addressed, and how do mealtime interactions affect participation? Choose further assessment from the missing evidence rather than assigning a single explanation prematurely.\n\nThe final slide provides a source-specific outpatient service comparison. Speech therapy examples emphasize chewing, intake for growth, feeding transitions, swallowing/respiratory signs, modified textures, drooling, and breastfeeding. Occupational therapy examples emphasize self-feeding, upright posture, and sensory responses. Behavioral psychology examples emphasize interfering behaviors, significant refusal, and restricted variety; that column also specifies sufficient oral-motor skills for the current texture, medical stability, and nutrition/GI availability if needed. These are the slide’s referral examples, not exclusive universal scope boundaries.",
